Eden: I opened a tiny PR to harmonize streaming snippets across Python/TS/Go and add a short retries note. Preview: https://github.com/redwood-inference/docs/pull/3789. Can folks sanity-check wording + edgecases?

Lian: Quick look 1 Python now uses an iterator:```python
for chunk in client.stream_chat(req):
    if chunk.hint == 'throttle':
        # optional caller backoff
        continue
    print(chunk.text, end='')
```
Add: call out that the throttle hint is advisory, not an automatic retry.

Marco: TS uses async iterator and shows both stream and paginated history so readers see the difference:```ts
for await (const d of client.chat.stream(req)) { render(d.delta) }

let page = await client.history.list()
while (page) { page.items.forEach(render); page = page.next_token ? await client.history.list({page_token: page.next_token}) : null }
```
One sentence: "Streams emit ordered deltas; pagination returns complete pages via next_token."

Eden: Good. For Go I simplified the loop to surface errors to caller:```go
stream, err := client.Stream(ctx, req)
for {
  chunk, ok := stream.Recv()
  if !ok { break }
  if chunk.Err != nil { continue }
  process(chunk.Text)
}
```
Note: respect ctx and avoid spawning unbounded goroutines per stream.

Lian: I also added a tiny pseudocode backoff block at the top (language-agnostic) so readers understand caller-level retries:```
if error == RATE_LIMIT:
  wait = base * 2**attempt
  sleep(wait)
  retry
```

